{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef fruit_distribution(s,n):\n    \"\"\"\n    In this task, you will be given a string that represents a number of apples and oranges\n    that are distributed in a basket of fruit this basket contains\n    apples, oranges, and mango fruits. Given the string that represents the total number of\n    the oranges and apples and an integer that represent the total number of the fruits\n    in the basket return the number of the mango fruits in the basket.\n    for examble:\n    fruit_distribution(\"5 apples and 6 oranges\", 19) ->19 - 5 - 6 = 8\n    fruit_distribution(\"0 apples and 1 oranges\",3) -> 3 - 0 - 1 = 2\n    fruit_distribution(\"2 apples and 3 oranges\", 100) -> 100 - 2 - 3 = 95\n    fruit_distribution(\"100 apples and 1 oranges\",120) -> 120 - 100 - 1 = 19\n    \"\"\"\n\n    apples = 0\n    oranges = 0\n\n    # Extract apples count\n    apple_match = re.search(r'(\\d+)\\s*apples', s)\n    if apple_match:\n        apples = int(apple_match.group(1))\n\n    # Extract oranges count\n    orange_match = re.search(r'(\\d+)\\s*oranges', s)\n    if orange_match:\n        oranges = int(orange_match.group(1))\n\n    # Calculate mangoes, ensuring a non-negative result\n    mangoes = n - apples - oranges\n    return max(0, mangoes)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "def pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest even value and its index in an array.\n    Returns [value, index] or an empty list if no even numbers or the array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    smallest_even_value = -1  # Sentinel value to indicate no even number found yet\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    for i, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even\n            if smallest_even_value == -1 or value < smallest_even_value:\n                # Found the first even value, or a new smaller even value\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = i\n            elif value == smallest_even_value and i < smallest_even_index:\n                # Found the same smallest even value but with a smaller index\n                smallest_even_index = i\n\n    if smallest_even_value == -1:\n        # No even numbers were found in the array\n        return []\n    else:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/64", "solution": "def vowels_count(s):\n    \"\"\"Write a function vowels_count which takes a string representing\n    a word as input and returns the number of vowels in the string.\n    Vowels in this case are 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'. Here, 'y' is also a\n    vowel, but only when it is at the end of the given word.\n\n    Example:\n    >>> vowels_count(\"abcde\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"ACEDY\")\n    3\n    >>> vowels_count(\"rhythm\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"brr\")\n    0\n    >>> vowels_count(\"sky\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"aeiou\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"AEIOU\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"eieio\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"happy\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"fly\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"mystery\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"system\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"mythology\")\n    3\n    >>> vowels_count(\"AbCdE\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"aCeDy\")\n    3\n    >>> vowels_count(\"\")\n    0\n    >>> vowels_count(\"a\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"y\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"b\")\n    0\n    >>> vowels_count(\"beautiful\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"ooooo\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"h3ll0!\")\n    0\n    >>> vowels_count(\"a.e.i.o.u\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"w0rd$w!th@y\")\n    1\n    \"\"\"\n    vowels = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'}\n    count = 0\n\n    # Convert to lowercase to handle case-insensitivity\n    s_lower = s.lower()\n\n    # Filter out non-alphabetic characters to align with 'word' definition\n    alphabetic_s = ''.join(filter(str.isalpha, s_lower))\n\n    if not alphabetic_s:\n        return 0\n\n    for i, char in enumerate(alphabetic_s):\n        if char in vowels:\n            count += 1\n        elif char == 'y' and i == len(alphabetic_s) - 1:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    '''\n    Given list of integers, return list in strange order.\n    Strange sorting, is when you start with the minimum value,\n    then maximum of the remaining integers, then minimum and so on.\n\n    Examples:\n    strange_sort_list([1, 2, 3, 4]) == [1, 4, 2, 3]\n    strange_sort_list([5, 5, 5, 5]) == [5, 5, 5, 5]\n    strange_sort_list([]) == []\n    '''\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    # Create a sorted copy of the list for efficient min/max extraction from ends.\n    mutable_list = sorted(lst)\n    result_list = []\n    take_min = True\n\n    while mutable_list:\n        if take_min:\n            # Append the current minimum element (first in sorted list).\n            result_list.append(mutable_list.pop(0))\n        else:\n            # Append the current maximum element (last in sorted list).\n            result_list.append(mutable_list.pop())\n        take_min = not take_min\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\ndef search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the greatest integer that is greater than zero and has a frequency\n    greater than or equal to its value. Returns -1 if no such value exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    from collections import Counter\n\n    # Calculate frequencies of all numbers in the list\n    counts = Counter(lst)\n\n    # Find all candidates that satisfy the condition\n    candidates = []\n    for num, freq in counts.items():\n        if freq >= num: # num is guaranteed to be positive, so num > 0 is implicit\n            candidates.append(num)\n\n    # If no candidates, return -1\n    if not candidates:\n        return -1\n\n    # Return the greatest among the candidates\n    return max(candidates)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/72", "solution": "```python\ndef will_it_fly(q,w):\n    '''\n    Write a function that returns True if the object q will fly, and False otherwise.\n    The object q will fly if it's balanced (it is a palindromic list) and the sum of its elements is less than or equal the maximum possible weight w.\n    '''\n    is_balanced = (q == q[::-1])\n    total_weight = sum(q)\n    return is_balanced and (total_weight <= w)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/77", "solution": "```python\ndef iscube(a):\n    '''\n    Write a function that takes an integer a and returns True\n    if this ingeger is a cube of some integer number.\n    Note: you may assume the input is always valid.\n    Examples:\n    iscube(1) ==> True\n    iscube(2) ==> False\n    iscube(-1) ==> True\n    iscube(64) ==> True\n    iscube(0) ==> True\n    iscube(180) ==> False\n    '''\n    if a == 0:\n        return True\n\n    # Determine the sign of the input number to handle negative cubes.\n    sign = 1 if a >= 0 else -1\n    abs_a = abs(a)\n\n    # Calculate the cube root using floating point and round to the nearest integer.\n    root = round(abs_a**(1/3))\n\n    # Re-apply the original sign to the root and check if its cube equals the original number.\n    return (sign * root) ** 3 == a\n```"}
