{"task_id": "HumanEval/73", "solution": "```python\ndef smallest_change(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array arr of integers, find the minimum number of elements that\n    need to be changed to make the array palindromic. A palindromic array is an array that\n    is read the same backwards and forwards. In one change, you can change one element to any other element.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    changes = 0\n    left = 0\n    right = n - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        if arr[left] != arr[right]:\n            changes += 1\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n    return changes\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "```python\ndef is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number x is a simple power of n.\n    x is a simple power of n if n**k = x for some non-negative integer k.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return x == 1\n    if n == 0:\n        return x == 0\n    if x == 1:\n        return True\n    if x <= 0:\n        return False\n\n    current_x = x\n    while current_x > 1:\n        if current_x % n != 0:\n            return False\n        current_x //= n\n    return current_x == 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle.\n    Otherwise return -1\n    Three sides make a valid triangle when the sum of any two sides is greater\n    than the third side.\n    '''\n    # Check if side lengths are positive\n    if not (a > 0 and b > 0 and c > 0):\n        return -1\n\n    # Use a small epsilon for floating-point comparisons to handle precision issues\n    # This ensures strict inequality as per \"sum of any two sides is greater than the third side.\"\n    EPSILON = 1e-9\n\n    # Check triangle inequality theorem\n    if not (a + b > c + EPSILON and\n            a + c > b + EPSILON and\n            b + c > a + EPSILON):\n        return -1\n\n    # Calculate semi-perimeter\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n\n    # Calculate area using Heron's formula\n    area_squared = s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)\n\n    # Handle potential floating-point inaccuracies where area_squared might be slightly negative\n    # If it's a very small negative value due to precision, it means the area is effectively zero.\n    if area_squared < 0:\n        area_squared = 0\n\n    area = math.sqrt(area_squared)\n\n    # Round the area to 2 decimal points\n    return round(area, 2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "```python\ndef is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Checks if 'a' is the product of three distinct prime numbers.\"\"\"\n    from itertools import combinations\n\n    def _is_prime(n):\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def _generate_primes_up_to(limit):\n        primes = []\n        for num in range(2, limit):\n            if _is_prime(num):\n                primes.append(num)\n        return primes\n\n    primes = _generate_primes_up_to(a)\n\n    for p1, p2, p3 in combinations(primes, 3):\n        if p1 * p2 * p3 == a:\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/78", "solution": "```python\ndef hex_key(num):\n    \"\"\"Counts the number of hexadecimal digits that are primes in a given string.\n    Prime hex digits are 2, 3, 5, 7, B (11), D (13).\"\"\"\n\n    prime_hex_decimal_values = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13}\n    prime_count = 0\n\n    for digit_char in num:\n        # Convert the hexadecimal digit character to its decimal integer value.\n        # This handles '0'-'9' and 'A'-'F' (uppercase) correctly.\n        decimal_value = int(digit_char, 16)\n\n        # Check if the decimal value is in the set of prime hexadecimal values.\n        if decimal_value in prime_hex_decimal_values:\n            prime_count += 1\n\n    return prime_count\n```\n"}
