{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "```python\ndef starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return the count of the numbers of n-digit\n    positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Calculate count of n-digit numbers that start with 1.\n    # This is 10^(n-1) for all positive n.\n    # For n=1, 10^(1-1) = 10^0 = 1 (number 1)\n    # For n=2, 10^(2-1) = 10^1 = 10 (numbers 10-19)\n    count_starts_with_1 = 10**(n - 1)\n\n    # Calculate count of n-digit numbers that end with 1.\n    # For n=1, it's 1 (number 1).\n    # For n>1, the first digit cannot be 0 (9 choices), and the n-2 middle digits\n    # can be any of 0-9 (10 choices each). So, 9 * 10^(n-2).\n    # For n=2, 9 * 10^(2-2) = 9 * 10^0 = 9 (numbers 11, 21, ..., 91)\n    # For n=3, 9 * 10^(3-2) = 9 * 10^1 = 90 (numbers 101, 111, ..., 991)\n    if n == 1:\n        count_ends_with_1 = 1\n    else:\n        count_ends_with_1 = 9 * (10**(n - 2))\n\n    # Calculate count of n-digit numbers that start AND end with 1.\n    # For n=1, it's 1 (number 1).\n    # For n=2, it's 1 (number 11).\n    # For n>2, the number is 1...1, with n-2 digits in between, each can be 0-9.\n    # So, 10^(n-2) choices for the middle digits.\n    # For n=3, 10^(3-2) = 10^1 = 10 (numbers 101, 111, ..., 191)\n    if n == 1:\n        count_starts_and_ends_with_1 = 1\n    elif n == 2:\n        count_starts_and_ends_with_1 = 1\n    else:\n        count_starts_and_ends_with_1 = 10**(n - 2)\n\n    # Apply the principle of inclusion-exclusion: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)\n    total_count = count_starts_with_1 + count_ends_with_1 - count_starts_and_ends_with_1\n\n    return total_count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_array(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array of non-negative integers, return a copy of the given array after sorting.\n    \"\"\"\n    copied_array = list(array)  # Create a shallow copy to avoid modifying the original.\n\n    if not copied_array or len(copied_array) == 1:\n        return copied_array\n\n    first_element = copied_array[0]\n    last_element = copied_array[-1]\n    sum_first_last = first_element + last_element\n\n    if sum_first_last % 2 != 0:  # Odd sum, sort ascending\n        return sorted(copied_array)\n    else:  # Even sum, sort descending\n        return sorted(copied_array, reverse=True)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/89", "solution": "```python\ndef encrypt(s):\n    \"\"\"Encrypts a string by rotating alphabetic characters by a shift of 4 positions.\n    Non-alphabetic characters are preserved, and rotation wraps around the alphabet.\n    \"\"\"\n    SHIFT_VALUE = 4\n    result = []\n    for char in s:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n            shifted_char = chr(((ord(char) - ord('a') + SHIFT_VALUE) % 26) + ord('a'))\n            result.append(shifted_char)\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z':\n            shifted_char = chr(((ord(char) - ord('A') + SHIFT_VALUE) % 26) + ord('A'))\n            result.append(shifted_char)\n        else:\n            result.append(char)\n    return \"\".join(result)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/81", "solution": "```python\ndef numerical_letter_grade(grades):\n    \"\"\"It is the last week of the semester and the teacher has to give the grades\n    to students. The teacher has been making her own algorithm for grading.\n    The only problem is, she has lost the code she used for grading.\n    She has given you a list of GPAs for some students and you have to write\n    a function that can output a list of letter grades using the following table:\n             GPA       |    Letter grade\n              4.0                A+\n            > 3.7                A\n            > 3.3                A-\n            > 3.0                B+\n            > 2.7                B\n            > 2.3                B-\n            > 2.0                C+\n            > 1.7                C\n            > 1.3                C-\n            > 1.0                D+\n            > 0.7                D\n            > 0.0                D-\n              0.0                E\n\n\n    Example:\n    grade_equation([4.0, 3, 1.7, 2, 3.5]) ==> ['A+', 'B', 'C-', 'C', 'A-']\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _convert_single_gpa(gpa: float) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Converts a single numerical GPA to its corresponding letter grade.\"\"\"\n        if gpa == 4.0:\n            return 'A+'\n        elif gpa > 3.7:\n            return 'A'\n        elif gpa > 3.3:\n            return 'A-'\n        elif gpa > 3.0:\n            return 'B+'\n        elif gpa > 2.7:\n            return 'B'\n        elif gpa > 2.3:\n            return 'B-'\n        elif gpa > 2.0:\n            return 'C+'\n        elif gpa > 1.7:\n            return 'C'\n        elif gpa > 1.3:\n            return 'C-'\n        elif gpa > 1.0:\n            return 'D+'\n        elif gpa > 0.7:\n            return 'D'\n        elif gpa > 0.0:\n            return 'D-'\n        else: # gpa is 0.0 or negative\n            return 'E'\n\n    converted_grades = []\n    for gpa_score in grades:\n        converted_grades.append(_convert_single_gpa(gpa_score))\n    return converted_grades\n```"}
